.TH UPSPIN 2
.SH NAME
upspin \- content-addressed store, signed directory and key server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyring.m";
include "upspin.m";
up := load Upspin Upspin->PATH;
Store, Dir, KeyServer, DirEntry, Block: import up;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Upspin
provides the pieces of an Upspin-style content name space; see
.IR upspin (1)
for the command and the overall model.
.PP
.B init
must be called before anything else.
.B store,
.B dir
and
.B keyserver
return handles rooted at a directory.
.PP
A
.B Store
holds content-addressed blocks.
.B Store.put
writes a block and returns its SHA-256 hex reference;
.B Store.get
reads a block and
.I rehashes
it, returning an error unless the bytes still match the reference.
.B putblocks
splits data into fixed
.B BLOCKSIZE
blocks and stores them;
.B getblocks
reassembles a block list, verifying each block on the way.
.PP
A
.B DirEntry
names content
.RB ( name ,
of the form
.IB user @ domain / path\fR)
and lists the
.B blocks
that make it up, with the owner's signature.
.B DirEntry.signedbytes
is the single canonical serialization that
.B signentry
and
.B verifyentry
both use; it covers the name and the ordered block references, so neither
can change under a valid signature.
.B Dir.put
and
.B Dir.lookup
store and retrieve entries under an owner-rooted name, rejecting any path
that would escape the owner's subtree;
.B Dir.glob
lists an owner's names.
.PP
A
.B KeyServer
maps
.IB user @ domain
to a public key.
.B verifyentry
takes the owner from the entry's
.I name,
looks its key up in the KeyServer, and checks the signature against it \(em
a key carried inside the entry is never trusted.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/upspin.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR upspin (1),
.IR keyring (2)
.SH BUGS
The backing stores are local directories, not
.IR styx (2)
services, and there is no encryption packing yet.
